# Metal‐Organic Frameworks for Gene Therapy and Detection

## abstract
Gene therapy holds great promise as a biomedicine for treating cancer, viral infection, and recessive gene disorders, while genetic detection has been used for disease diagnosis and prevention. The appropriate carrier is crucial to improve the delivery efficiency of nucleic acids. Although viral and non-viral delivery vectors have been developed, their poor nucleic acid loading efficiency, off-target toxicity, and immunogenicity significantly hamper their use. Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have attracted a lot of attention owing to their high surface areas and tunable structures. Recently, nano-metric MOFs are shown to be promising nonviral vectors in gene delivery and detection although at an early stage of development. This review focuses on highlighting the current status and promising breakthroughs of MOF use in nucleic acid delivery, including siRNA, miRNA and mRNA delivery, DNA gene delivery and transfection, as well as probes for detection of gene expression. This study will help promote the clinical transformation and application of gene modulation, gene delivery, and detection that use of MOFs can offer.

Gene therapy holds great promise as a biomedicine for treating cancer, viral infection, and recessive gene disorders, while genetic detection has been used for disease diagnosis and prevention. The appropriate carrier is crucial to improve the delivery efficiency of nucleic acids. Although viral and nonviral delivery vectors have been developed, their poor nucleic acid loading efficiency, off-target toxicity, and immunogenicity significantly hamper their use. Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have attracted a lot of attention owing to their high surface areas and tunable structures. Recently, nanometric MOFs are shown to be promising nonviral vectors in gene delivery and detection although at an early stage of development. This review focuses on highlighting the current status and promising breakthroughs of MOF use in nucleic acid delivery, including siRNA, miRNA and mRNA delivery, DNA gene delivery and transfection, as well as probes for detection of gene expression. This study will help promote the clinical transformation and application of gene modulation, gene delivery, and detection that use of MOFs can offer.
## 1. Introduction
Gene therapy is a collective term for the manipulation of nucleic acids (DNA or RNA) in the genetic code to promote cure or prevention of human disorders.[1] Over the last few decades, gene therapy has exhibited huge promise in biomedicine for treating cancer as well as recessive gene disorders such as muscular dystrophy, cystic fibrosis, hemophilia, and sickle cell anemia.[2] Gene therapy to address virus infections is also gaining momentum with the most recent significant successful use of mRNA vaccines to treat COVID-19, a SARS-CoV-2 infection.[3] Gene detection can be used to diagnose diseases as well as to predict risk factors for disease prevention.[4] For example, gene detection has been widely used in the detection of neonatal hereditary diseases and risk assessment of cancers.[4a,c,5] Despite this great interest in gene delivery to diagnose and treat disease, the site specific and efficient delivery of oligo and polynucleic acids faces many challenges.[6] In particular, rapid enzymatic degradation, poor cellular uptake, low transfection efficiency, and nonspecific biodistribution of genetic materials hinder the extensive clinical applications of gene therapy.[6b–d,7]
The progress of gene therapy and detection is greatly hindered by the inadequacy of delivery carriers. Abundant research has been carried out to develop vehicles for improving the delivery efficiency of nucleic acids.[8] Although the development of viral and nonviral delivery vectors has made progress, low cellular uptake, poor endolysosomal escape, poor systemic circulation, and uncontrolled release remain the challenges to overcome for more effective DNA/RNA delivery.[6c] Adenoviruses and lentiviruses have been widely used preclinically and clinically as gene therapy vectors due to their high transfection efficacy. The restricted gene length carrying capacity, inherent immunogenicity, and pathogenicity have limited their use in the clinic.[6a,7,9] Although, the use of adenovirus delivery of the Oxford–AstraZeneca COVID-19 vaccine (AZD1222) shows these limitations can be overcome for the right clinical indication. Nonviral vectors have received extensive attention, particularly with respect to potential for lower toxicity, less immunogenicity, ease of modification, and lower cost compared to the viral vectors.[6a] In this regard, various nano-structured nonviral vectors have been synthesised, notably liposomes, gold-based nanomaterials, aggregates (micelles), dendrimers and other polymeric organics, as well as silicates.[7] However, there are still many challenges that impede the practical application of non-viral vectors, including poor stability, low loading capability, insufficient cellular uptake, limited gene expression.[6a,10]
In order to address these challenges, much effort has been devoted to developing new nonviral vectors for efficient delivery of genetic materials.[6a,c,7]
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), formed by metal ions and organic ligands, have attracted increasing attention in the gene therapy and genetic detection fields.[11] This study originated with the detection of biomarkers using MOF-based sensors.[6a,11d,12] Compared with the traditional carriers, MOFs possess unique advantages for delivery of nucleic acids: First, the versatile and tunable structure of MOFs is beneficial to obtaining materials with the desired morphologies, sizes, aperture, compositions, and chemical properties relevant to the capture of nucleic acids,[13] facilitating protection of nucleic acids from enzymatic degradation.[7,14] Second, high porosities and large surface area endow MOFs with potential for high loading capacity of DNA/RNA.[15] Whilst the large surface and chemical groups of a MOF facilitate functionalization with chemical moieties that can promote nucleic acid uptake by cells.[7,16] Another advantage of MOF modification is that this can enable targeted and controlled release of the loaded nucleic acids.[6c,17] Thirdly, MOFs can be used to facilitate escape of nucleic acids from endo/lysosomes thereby improving the transfection efficiency.[6a,11b] Fourthly, MOFs can be prepared from organics that have good biocompatibility and biodegradability consistent with the need for their safe use in vivo.[6a,18] In short, MOFs show great potential to encapsulate, deliver, and protect nucleic acids efficaciously without chemical modification of the payload.[14,19]
This review will focus on highlighting the current status and promising breakthroughs of MOF use in nucleic acid delivery, including siRNA, miRNA, and mRNA delivery, DNA gene delivery and transfection, and probes for detection of gene expression. By focusing on the delivery of nucleic acids, it is anticipated that this review will promote the clinical transformation and application of gene modulation, delivery, and detection that use of MOFs can offer (Figure 1).
## 2. Characteristics of MOFs Used for Gene Therapy and Detection
## 2.1. Molecular Structure and Porosity of MOFs
MOFs are a new type of coordination polymer constituted by organic ligands and metal ions.[20] Many kinds of MOFs have been reported for nucleic acid delivery, MOF materials used in gene therapy can be classified according to their make-up and physicochemical properties (Table 1). The organic ligand/metal skeleton and the resulting structure, specific surface area, and pore size will affect the encapsulation and therefore delivery efficiency of nucleic acids used as drugs or probes. These MOFs can be analysed and compared according to their structure, specific surface area, and pore size. The porous structure and large surface area of MOFs endow these structures with high loading capacity for nucleic acids. The specific surface area of MOFs used for gene therapy is usually more than $1000\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (Table 1) which is comparable to mesoporous silica nanoparticles with a specific surface area of $600{-}1100\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ .[21] The nucleic acids can be encapsulated inside the MOF cavity with a sufficiently large pore size, or loaded onto the MOF surface via multiple organic/metal ion interaction sites.[6a,11a,b,22] Specifically, the pore size of MOFs used for nucleic acid delivery is from 0.8 to $3\ \mathrm{nm}$ . Nucleic acid monomers are ${\approx}2~\mathrm{nm}$ in diameter and oligonucleotides vary in length from a few nanometers to several hundred nanometers, while a human mRNA will be typically $2~\mathrm{kb}$ and forming a secondary/tertiary structure considerably greater in volume than a linear polynucleotide. Hence, in principle, the pore size of the MOF needs to be larger than $2\mathrm{nm}$ to accommodate oligo/polynucleotides into the MOF cavity. However, in practice, MOFs with pore sizes less than $2\:\mathrm{nm}$ (e.g., see Table  1: ZIF-8, $1.16\ \mathrm{nm}$ and UiO-66, $0.8\ \mathrm{nm}$ ) can also deliver nucleic acid molecules efficiently, indicating that MOFs can load nucleic acids in ways not reliant on pore size. Typically, most of the MOF particles used for gene therapy and detection are in the range of 100–500 nanometers. Naked RNAs or DNAs are difficult to move across cell membranes due to their fragile nature and charged property. Hence nanoscale MOFs can protect nucleic acids from degradation and facilitate their cellular uptake through the endocytosis pathway.[7,22,23] Dissociation of MOF metal ions (e.g., Zr, Fe, Zn ions) could also disturb the lysosome structure, facilitating RNA/DNA escape by binding to the negatively charged lysosome membrane.[11b,15,22] Moreover, because nucleic acids are negatively charged, functionalization of MOFs with cationic material has been shown to improve delivery and transfection efficiency due to the increased electrostatic interactions between cargo and carrier.[7,24]
## 2.2. Interactions between MOFs and Nucleic Acids
MOFs are generally designed and validated as versatile nanocarriers for accommodating nucleic acids as cargo through $"/"$ indicates that there is no mention in the reference.
coordination bonds,[22,28] electrostatic interactions,[11c,23,28b] van der Waals interactions,[13] or covalent conjugation.[26,29] More specifically, siRNAs can bind to the surface of MOFs through coordination bonds between metal sites of MOFs and phosphate residues on the backbone of nucleic acids.[11b,22] MOFs can also load siRNA and nucleic acid probes owing to strong electrostatic interactions.[11c,23] Similarly, the negatively charged cytosine-phosphate-guanosine (CpG) oligodeoxynucleotides were adsorbed onto ZIF-8 via electrostatic interaction.[28b] Studies have reported that dibenzylcyclooctyne (DBCO) functionalized nucleic acid can be conjugated to azide functionalized MOFs by the “click-chemistry” strategy (Figure 2D).[26,29b]
In an interesting study, ssDNA was incorporated into the pores of four kinds of Ni-IRMOF-74 through van der Waals interactions.[13] This approach demonstrates that appropriate interactions between ssDNA and MOFs are needed to retain ssDNA inside the cavity of MOFs. Also that while the binding interaction should be enough to capture the ssDNA, it also should be sufficiently weak for it to subsequently release the cargo. This study also showed that this MOF/DNA interaction increased as the pore size of the MOF was extended.[13] Specifically, as the length of organic linkers increased, the pore sizes of Ni-IRMOF-74-II to -V were fine-tuned from 2.2 to $4.2\ \mathrm{nm}$ , respectively (Figure 2A,B). To visualise ssDNA uptake of MOFs, the $5^{\prime}$ end of ssDNA was fluorescently labelled with 6-carboxyfluorescein and sequestration of ssDNA into the MOF pores resulted in fluorescence quenching. All four members of Ni-IRMOF-74-II-V could incorporate ssDNA into their pore structure as confirmed by almost complete loss of fluorescent labelled ssDNA from solution in the presence of the respective MOF (Figure  2C). The study also showed that the Ni-MOF-74 with a $1.5\mathrm{nm}$ pore size was insufficient to allow ssDNA loading. As for the release process, $55.0\%$ of loaded ssDNA was released from Ni-IRMOF-74-II, while Ni-IRMOF-74-III to -V with larger pore sizes exhibited less release $24.7\%$ , $17.8\%$ , $20.2\%$ , respectively),[13] indicating that MOFs with larger pore size hindered DNA release most likely due to the strength of interaction. The favourable release profile of Ni-IRMOF-74-II demonstrated that moderate interactions between MOFs and ssDNA are suitable for the uptake, protection, and release of DNA cargos.
## 3. MOFs for RNA Delivery
The current focus on further development of RNA-based gene therapies is design of delivery systems that prevent systemic degradation of RNA, and to realize site-specific delivery.[1b,30] In recent years, nanoparticles (NPs) have been designed to improve the efficacy of RNA-based therapy.[31] Specifically,the pore size, particle size, morphology, and chemical properties of MOFs can be adjusted through selecting organic ligands/metal ions and controlling the synthesis conditions. This tunability is beneficial to the encapsulation of different cargos by MOFs.[32] In this regard, MOFs are promising carriers for RNA delivery owing to their precision tunability and high surface areas.[6a,24]
## 3.1. MOFs for siRNA and miRNA Delivery
RNA interference (RNAi) has become an effective technique for sequence-specific gene silencing, which possesses great potentials for gene-targeted therapy.[11b] Small interfering RNAs (siRNAs) are double-stranded RNA, a typically 20–24 (normally 21) base pairs in length which can down-regulate gene expression by degrading mRNA and preventing  translation. The siRNAs have been widely explored as therapeutics for human disease. A leading example is the GalNAc–siRNA conjugates which are effective for siRNA delivery to liver hepatocytes with potential for targeting other tissues.[33] Patisiran, a hepatic transthyretin-targeted siRNA and Givosiran directed against 5-aminolevulinic acid synthase to cure acute hepatic porphyria are both clinically approved GalNAc–siRNA conjugates. However, the susceptibility to enzymatic degradation, limited endosomal escape, off-target effects, and rapid blood clearance of RNA is an impediment to their clinical use.[34]
MOFs represent effective delivery vehicles for improving bioavailability and biocompatibility of siRNA.[23] A successful encapsulation of native siRNA into a ${\mathrm{Zr}}^{4+}.$ /TBAPy4-MOF (NU1000) demonstrated that siRNA-MOF complexation protected siRNA from enzymatic degradation and supported effective gene knockdown. In this study, RFP mCherry, as a model probe of cell gene expression, was significantly decreased following treatment with siRNA/MOF.[14] Use of selenium/ruthenium (Se/Ru) modified MIL-101(Fe) nanoparticles for delivering siRNAs was shown to improve treatment efficiency through silencing multiple drug resistance (MDR) siRNA in MCF-7/T (Taxol-resistance) cells. $\mathrm{Se/Ru}@\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}101$ achieved the silencing of MDR genes by protecting MDR gene-silencing siRNAs against nuclease degradation, enhancing siRNA cellular uptake and escape from endosomes/lysosom. Xenograft tumor volume in the Se@MIL-101-siRNA group was decreased by nearly $80\%$ when compared to an untreated group.[11b]
Clinical trials have repeatedly displayed that nucleic acidbased therapeutics are more effective when used in combination with drugs that work through complementary mechanisms including immune checkpoint inhibitors,[35] chemotherapeutics, photodynamic therapy,[31b] and radiotherapy.[36] In this regard, MOFs hold great promise in the co-delivery of gene therapy with other therapeutics as combination treatments. For example, nanoscale UiO MOFs could encapsulate a cisplatin prodrug, and also loaded with MDR gene-silencing siRNAs by surface coordination at the same time (Figure 3C). Such UiO MOFs enhanced the stability of siRNAs and promoted siRNA escape from endosomes resulting in MDR gene silencing. Co-delivery of cisplatin and siRNAs using MOF markedly enhanced the chemotherapeutic efficacy through combination of reversing cisplatin resistance and silencing MDR genes.[22] Wang group reported a polydopamine-ZIF-8 nanoplatform (PSZ) for siRNA delivery combined with photothermal therapy in vivo (Figure  3A). RT-PCR and WB analysis demonstrated that delivery of PSZsiRNA resulted in $70\%$ inhibition of Bcl-2 expression compared with the untreated group (Figure 3B). In addition, the polydopamine constituent could simultaneously serve as a non-invasive imaging contrast agent and photothermal agent. In summary, this multifunctional nanoplatform realized an enhanced therapeutic efficacy by combining gene therapy with photothermal therapy and incorporating photoacoustic/near infrared dualmodal imaging to guide treatment.[6b]
MOF nanoplatform coated with platelet cell membrane illustrates the use of biomimetics to facilitate tumour cell internalization of siRNA payloads.[23] In this study, siRNAs were loaded inside porous ZIF-8 MOF nanoparticles through a modified in situ biomineralization method giving rise to a high loading efficiency. And then the siRNA-loaded MOFs were coated by platelet membrane (Figure 3D). In vitro and in vivo experiments demonstrated that the resulting biomimetic nanoformulation was able to effectively silence gene expression of survivin, a therapeutically relevant target, and significantly control tumor growth (Figure 3E).
MicroRNAs (miRNAs) are another kind of small RNAs used for RNA interference and have similar properties to siRNAs. MicroRNAs are single-stranded non-coding RNA’s of ${\widetilde{\mathbf{\Gamma}}}{\approx}22$ nucleotides. There are over 1800 miRNAs in the human genome targeting mRNAs and play an important role in control of cell proliferation, differentiation, apoptosis, and oncogenesis.[6a,37] Antisense molecules can block oncogenic miRNAs for cancer treatment[31b] although development of miRNA therapeutics is impeded by the delivery challenge.[31b,38] Given the capability of MOF-based nanoplatforms for nucleic acid capture, they are an attractive approach for miRNA delivery.
An iron (III) carboxylate MIL MOF were applied to encapsulate an oncosupressor miR-145 and compare benefit with a non-specific siRNA as a control. The properties of MOFs were affected by milieu pH which in turn can influence the interactions between RNA and the carrier nanoMOF. Thus, the effects of pH on the particle size and potential of MOFs were investigated and the optimal pH values were selected to encapsulate RNA. In vitro transfection data indicated that the obtained MOF complexes were able to deliver and enhance the cellular expression of miRNA-145.[6a]
The ZIF-8 MOFs (Figure 3F) were explored as alternatives to viral vectors to achieve efficient intracellular delivery of miRNA. ZIF-8 was shown to protect a miR-34a mimic from digestion.
The released miR-34a-m was able to bind to complementary target mRNA, inhibit mRNA translation (Figure  3G) and protein expression of Bcl-2, all of which supports its use in gene therapy. In vitro and in vivo experiments demonstrated a higher antitumor effect of miR- $34\mathrm{a}.\mathrm{m}@\mathrm{ZIF}.8$ than MOF-free miR$34\mathrm{a}.\mathrm{m}$ by combining gene delivery and chemodynamic therapy (CDT) with ZIF-8-induced ROS. This MOF-based delivery system paves a new way for RNA delivery and combined gene/ chemodynamic therapy (Table 2).[6c]
## 3.2. MOFs for mRNA Delivery
In principle, tissue targeted delivery of messenger RNA (mRNA) represents a most promising therapeutic approach for transcriptional modulation of all genetic diseases and also for vaccine production.[39] The mRNA is expressed directly in the cell cytoplasm hence does not require transport through the nuclear membrane, a significant challenge for DNA-based therapeutics.[39b,40] The challenge is the intrinsic processes involved in mRNA degradation as part of the overall control of protein synthesis. Compared with siRNAs $({\approx}14\ \mathrm{kDa})$ , the size of mRNA molecules is significantly larger $(300{-}5000~\mathrm{~kDa})$ , which makes mRNA delivery even more challenging.[39b,40] Therefore, the success of mRNA therapy requires technologies that can efficiently package kDa sized polynucleotides for cytoplasmic delivery. A dendritic UiO-66-Zr-MOF nanoplatform has such a capability. The Zr-MOF was modified with polycationic ethanolamine conjugated poly (glycidyl methacrylate) [PGMA (EA)]. The MOF-[PGMA (EA)] markedly improved the colloidal stability of the mRNA assembly and protected mRNA from degradation (Figure 4A). Importantly, this MOF[PGMA (EA)] entrapped mRNA facilitated higher cellular mRNA uptake (Figure 4B) and 20-fold higher gene expression compare to the linear [PGMA (EA)] component per se. Notably, the mRNA transfection efficiency of MOF-[PGMA (EA)] was higher than that of commercially available nucleic acids transfection systems including jet PEI, Fugene $\mathrm{\DeltaHD^{TM}}$ , CycloFectTM (Figure 4C).[24]
## 4. MOFs for DNA transfection Delivery
## 4.1. MOFs for Plasmid DNA Delivery
A silica-metal-organic framework hybrid nanoparticle (SMOF NP) (Figure 4D) was designed to deliver nucleic acids (mRNA and DNA). The SMOF NP encapsulated nucleic acids with high loading efficiency $(>90\%)$ and high loading content $(>9\mathrm{\wt.}\%)$ .[15] Nucleic acid-loaded SMOF NPs showed a higher or similar transfection efficiency compared to the commercial transfection agent Lipofectamine 2000 (Lipo 2000) in different cells (Figure  4E). This stable and multifunctional SMOF NP is a promising carrier for gene therapy (Table 3).[15]
DNA delivery is challenging because transport into the nucleus requires traversing of the nuclear membrane, but MOF carriers have been demonstrated to improve the DNA transfection efficiency. DNA can induce MOF formation via a biomimetic mineralization process. Biomimetic mineralization is a method for encapsulating bioactive substances within protective exteriors by adopting a self-assembly process.[41] The biomimetic mineralization of MOFs forms a nanoporous shell, which encapsulates and protects DNA from degradation.[28a] This is exemplified by a facile one-pot strategy to encapsulate plasmid
DNA (pDNA) expressing enhanced green fluorescent protein (pEGFP-C1) into ZIF-8 through biomimetic mineralization and coprecipitation. The pEGFP-C1 $@$ ZIF-8 nanostructure functionalized with branched polyethyleneimine (PEI) exhibited higher loading capacity, better cellular uptake, and higher gene expression than free pDNA, which could be due to the increased electrostatic interaction between cationic PEI and pDNA (Figure 5A). The ZIF-8 MOF-based vectors protected the pDNA against enzymatic degradation and reached ${\approx}90\%$ transfection efficacy in vitro.[7] Similarly, nano ZIF-8 MOFs were utilized to carry a green fluorescent protein plasmid (plGFP) (Figure 5B), the genetic expression of plGFP demonstrated that MOFs can maintain the integrity and function of DNA.[9]
MOF NP was also reported to encapsulate pDNA with high loading efficiency $(>90\%)$ .[15] In another study, a core-shell UiO-PGMA-EA catiomer was synthesized for gene delivery. A $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOF consisting of ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2^{-}}{\mathrm{UiO}}{\cdot}66$ was elaborated as the core NP and functionalized with PGMA. The UiO-PGMA-EA nanohybrids were obtained through reaction with EA, which condensed pDNA via electrostatic interactions. Moreover, UiO-PGMA-EA was shown to protect the encapsulated pDNA and improve transfection activity. This work supports the MOFfunctionalized material as promising platform for design of ideal gene delivery systems.[42]
A recent study reported a magnetic core coated by MIL-100 (Fe) shell and the nanoparticles were further modified with PEI to immobilize pDNA. The designed MOF nanoplatforms delivered pDNAs of different structure and length $(4.7\mathrm{-}10.7~\mathrm{kb})$ 1 on the MOF surface. At the same time, these nanoplatforms maintained the porosity of the MOF which remained available for encapsulation of small molecules. In vitro and in vivo results demonstrated that this multifunctional MOF-based system achieved the co-delivery of gene and a small molecules, showing a great potential for clinical applications.[43]
## 4.2. MOFs for Linear DNA Delivery
Ni-IRMOF-74 with pore size from 2.2 to $4.2\ \mathrm{nm}$ was used to entrap single strand DNA (ssDNA, 11–53 nucleotides) (Figure 6A). Two Ni-IRMOF-74 exhibited a $92\%$ transfection efficiency in mouse $\mathrm{CD4+}$ T immune cells and $30\%$ transfection efficiency in human THP-1 immune cells. This was much higher than that of the commercially available transfectant agents Neofect and Lipo. However, their practical applications may be restricted by the toxicity of Ni-compounds.[13] With regard ZIF-8 NP, the tumor-targeted enrichment of DNA payloads may be facilitated through an EPR effect, increasing the cellular uptake of DNA.[44] DNA release from ZIF-8 could be mediated through dissociation of coordinated $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}.$ imidazole ligands in the tumor acidic microenvironment.[25]
DNAzymes are single-stranded DNA oligonucleotides which can catalyze the cleavage of DNA or RNA.[11c,45] Based on divalent cation-dependent and sequence-specific features, DNAzymes have attracted increasing attention in medicine applications.[11c,45a,46] Despite the interest in DNAzyme-mediated gene silencing, the challenge is overcoming poor cellular delivery as well as ineffective intracellular DNAzyme cleavage originating from insufficient cofactor supply.[25,45a] In this regard, MOFs have been used as promising nanocarriers for effective delivery of DNAzyme.[11c]
A 33-nucleotide DNAzyme was designed with a sequence targeted to human early growth response-1 (EGR-1) mRNA that can be over-expressed in MCF-7 cells. Ni-IRMOF-74-II successfully delivered this DNAzyme into MCF-7 cells and produced $76\%$ inhibition of EGR-1 gene expression. In contrast, delivery of this DNAzyme by Lipo and Neofect inhibited $17\%$ and $32\%$ , respectively.[13] An example of a theranostic DNAzyme approach was use of ZIF-8 nanoparticles to encapsulate chlorin e6 (Ce6)-functionalized DNAzyme for imagingguided combined gene therapy and photodynamic therapy (Figure  6B). The Ce6-DNAzyme $@$ ZIF-8 significantly suppressed tumor growth in MCF-7 tumor-bearing mice via the co-delivery of photosensitizer Ce6 and functional DNA. Ce6- DNAzyme $@$ ZIF-8 nanosystem exhibited efficient intracellular delivery and released Ce6-DNAzyme in a pH-responsive manner, with the released $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ ions supplying an adequate amount of cofactor for activating the DNAzyme mediated gene inhibition of EGR-1 mRNA. Concomitantly, the Ce6 provides both an effective image guidance signal and singlet oxygen mediated photodynamic therapy.[25]
A further example of DNA delivery is CpG oligodeoxynucleotides complexed with UiO- $66\small{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ (Figure 6C). The release of CpG was greatly enhanced at acid $\mathrm{\pH}$ while negligible in neutral or alkaline conditions. The CpG-MOFs accumulated in macrophage acidic organelles resulting in release of immunostimulant CpG oligonucleotides.[19] ZIF-8 nanomaterials were utilized to encapsulate ovalbumin (OVA) antigens and the obtained OVA $@$ ZIF-8 adsorbed CpG oligodeoxynucleotides via strong electrostatic interactions providing a vaccine adjuvant. The OVA $@$ ZIF-8-CpG elicited potent cellular and humoral immune responses and also a strong immune memory response. This study supports the potential use of MOF-based platforms for vaccine administration.[28b]
In another study, a core-shell structured nanoplatform was synthesized through self-assembly of the antisense oligonucleotide (G3139), doxorubicin (DOX), and Fe (II) ions followed by surface mineralization with a thin shell of ZIF-8 (Figure  6D). Both the drug loading efficiency of G3139 and DOX were greater than $80\%$ through optimizing the loading conditions. The final DOX/Fe-G3139 $@$ ZIF-8 nanoplatform achieved efficient co-delivery of its payload and exhibited an augmented anticancer effect. The simultaneous delivery of nucleic acids and other drugs will become more feasible owing to the convenience of biomimetic strategy.[11a]
In summary, MOFs have been successfully used to increase cellular uptake of DNA and improve its transfection efficiency. Importantly, MOF constituents possess multiple functions in addition to loading nucleic acids. For example, MOF nanocarriers were demonstrated to deliver therapeutic DNAzyme and supply requisite cofactors (such as ${\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ ) for activating effective gene therapy. Notably, MOFs can co-deliver nucleic acids and other therapeutics to achieve combination therapy such as chemo/gene therapy, gene/photothermal therapy.
## 4.3. MOFs Surface Functionalization for DNA Delivery
Manipulation of the surface functionality of MOFs to attach and orientate oligonucleotides has enabled programmable and sequence-specific DNA delivery.[47] Such nucleic acid-functionalized MOFs have application in gene and immunostimulatory oligonucleotide delivery as well as biosensors.[27b,48]
A series of DNA-MOF nanoparticles with high loading density and highly oriented surface DNA are now described. $[26,472,49]$ Initially, an azide functionalised MOF $(\mathrm{UiO-}66{\cdot}\mathrm{N}_{3}$ ) was employed to covalently surface attach oligonucleotides using strained-alkyne click chemistry (Figure 7A). The resulting nucleic acid-MOF conjugates exhibited increased stability and enhanced cellular uptake when compared with unfunctionalized MOF particles. This work was the first to create a new and modular class of nanostructure where both the nature of the oligonucleotide layer and MOF composition could be useful as nucleic acid delivery systems.[26]
DNA tethered to the external surfaces of MOF particles has also been carried out using metal−phosphate coordination of terminal phosphate-modified oligonucleotides. In this way, various MOFs representing different metal nodes (Zr, Cr, Fe, and Al), organic ligands, and five topologies, i.e., UiO-66, UiO-67-bpy, UiO-68-N3, PCN-222, PCN-223, PCN-224, MIL-101 (Al), MIL-101 (Fe), and MIL-101 $(\mathrm{Cr})$ were all successfully surface functionalized with DNA. The structural integrity and porosity of the MOF architecture was shown to be preserved after DNA functionalization.[50] This study demonstrates a method of DNA-MOF preparation with general applicability. The MOF-DNA product was a stable and programmable supporting the use of MOFs as nanoparticles with potential use in biomedicine.[47a,50]
In a separate study, PCN-224 MOF nanoparticles were functionalized by a DNA (30-mer poly A) via mulriple coordination interactions between phosphate backbone of DNA and $Z\mathrm{r}6$ clusters.[27b] Results showed that the DNA attached to MOFs retained their capability to hybridize to the complementary DNA targets. Thus, the DNA-MOFs have been demonstrated for therapeutic DNA delivery. For instance, CpG oligodeoxyribonucleotide with a $\ensuremath{\mathrm{T}}30$ tail was linked to the A30 oligonucleotides functionalized MOFs via DNA hybridization. The CpG–MOFs can efficiently enhance immunostimulatory activity in vitro and in vivo, further demonstrating the potential the use of MOFs to deliver biomedicines (Figure 7B–D) (Table 4).[27b]
## 5. MOFs for Delivery of Nucleic Acid Probes
MOFs have attracted considerable attention as biosensors for disease diagnosis and pathology due to their large surface area, controllable structure, and facile functionalization. In this regard, MOFs have been used to deliver nucleic acid probes and DNA amplification for detection use with high sensitivity, good reproducibility, and stability.[51]
There is significant interest in the miRNAs as clinical biomarkers for detection of cancers, diabetes, and cardiovascular disease.[37] ZIF-8 was applied to deliver a nucleic acid probe for imaging miRNA-21 expression (Figure 8A). In brief, a Cy5- labeled DNAzyme strand and a Cy3-labeled substrate strand including a complementary segment to miRNA-21 was prepared as a duplex FRET probe. Then, the duplex probe was adsorbed onto of ZIF-8 nanocarriers via electrostatic interaction. As previously described, ZIF-8 could afford protection for nucleic acid probes, improve cellular uptake and promote endolysosomal escape of probes. After cell internalization, degradation of ZIF-8 in acidic endosomes led to the release of nucleic acid probes as well as ${\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ ions, the divalent metal ions serving as a necessary DNAzyme cofactor. Cellular miRNA-21 can hybridize with the complementary segment of the DNAzyme strand resulting in its dissociation from the MOFdelivered duplex probe, changing the $\mathrm{Cy}3/\mathrm{Cy}5$ FRET signal (Figure  8B). This approach was used to measure miRNA-21 expression in different cells.[11c]
DNA amplification using non-PCR based mRNA detection is proving a highly effective approach for low-abundance biomarker analysis. The UiO-66 MOF was used to enhance the intracellular uptake and enzymatic stability of DNA amplifiers for detecting and imaging multiple low-expression mRNAs. In the presence of a target mRNA, the DNA amplifier can be released by a competitive reaction between cellular phosphate ions and $\mathrm{zr}$ sites of MOF, producing an intense fluorescence signal. In this way, a UiO-66 DNA amplifier was effective at detecting TK1 and survivin mRNA expression levels with high sensitivity and specificity in HepG2 and HL7702 cells (Figure 8C).[6d]
For rapid detection of cancer biomarkers, a “three-in-one” fluorescence biosensor was described using a Zr-MOF as a template for in situ synthesis of Au NPs, and further coated by an ultrathin graphene oxide (GO) layer to form $\mathsf{M O F@A u N P@}$ GO. The resulting $\mathrm{MOF@AuNP@GO}$ exhibited a strong binding affinity and fluorescence quenching capacity for labeled ssDNAs and aptamers, which were designed as “no-wash” fluorescence detection probes for the p53 gene, BRCA1 gene and prostate specific antigen protein (Figure 8D and Table 5). This method enhanced the signal-to-noise ratio and reliability while shortening the analysis time using the ratiometric fluorescence signal detection approach.[27a]
## 6. MOF use for CRISPR co-delivery of sgRNA and Cas9
CRISPR/Cas9, containing a Cas9 nuclease and an engineered single guide RNA (sgRNA), has become a dominant technique for genome editing. The therapeutic focus of CRISPR is gene therapy of cancer, infection, and genetic disorders.[52] To act as a functional therapeutic, CRISPR needs to deliver both a large size Cas9 protein and highly charged sgRNA. In addition, CRISPR endolysosomal entrapment is a necessary feature of CRISPR cell accumulation. The subsequent endolysosomal escape to enable uptake into the nucleus is a key delivery challenge for this therapeutic platform. MOF nanoparticles have proven effective for this purpose with a zinc-imidazolate MOF (ZIF-8) used to successfully encapsulate both the large Cas9 protein and the anionic sgRNA with a $17\%$ loading efficiency.[40] The protonation of the imidazole based framework in the acidic endosome is suggested to promote endolysosomal escape of Cas9/sgRNA for nucleus uptake. The gene editing capability of ZIF-8 encapsulated CRISPR/Cas9 was verified by demonstrating a GFP gene knock down of $37\%$ .[53]
## 7. Conclusion and Perspective
MOFs have been used successfully to deliver RNA and DNA in laboratory research both for gene delivery and detection in cellular systems. Numerous studies have demonstrated that RNA-MOF complexation can protect RNA from enzymatic degradation, facilitate its cell uptake and gene therapeutic efficiency. MOFs were also shown to achieve efficient delivery of DNA, displaying a similar or higher transfection efficiency compared with Lipofectamine 2000. Importantly, MOFs were reported to co-deliver oligonucleotides and other therapeutics working on different mechanisms, which paves a new way for combined gene therapy. In addition, MOFs have been used to deliver nucleic acid probes for detection of biomarkers. However, the application of MOFs for delivery of nucleic acids is still in the early development stage. There are still formidable challenges in the adoption of MOF-based delivery systems for clinical use.
Toxicity is the first consideration for clinical transformation of MOFs. The toxic effects of MOFs are likely due to their chemical composition, i.e., metal ions and non-physiological organic ligands. Exposure to Cu, Cd, and Mn containing MOFs may cause serious health concerns in humans.[54] Terephthalic acid, a MOF organic linker component is associated with irritation to the respiratory tract, eye, and skin.[55] In addition, solvents or additives used in MOF synthesis interacting with biomolecules could contribute to the toxicity of MOFs observed. However, recently, the creation of MOFs that are “safe-and-sustainableby-design” has been proposed that could utilise biocompatible building blocks to foster the production of therapeutically safe MOFs. For example, selecting endogenous ligands such as nucleobases,[56] amino acids,[57] or peptides,[58] and metal ions with high biocompatibility like Fe and Ca is identified as highly beneficial in minimizing MOF toxicity profile.[59] To date, the in vitro and short-term/acute in vivo toxicity of MOFs is mainly described but long-term toxicity has rarely been reported.[60] Studies reported that a Cu-MOF (HKUST-1) suspension was toxic to both embryos and adult zebrafish following $24\mathrm{h}$ of exposure. Specifically, Cu-MOF was associated with a malformation in hatching embryos, affecting the hatching rate, survival, and embryogenesis. The estimated $96\mathrm{~h~}$ half-lethal concentrations $\left(\mathrm{LC}_{50}\right)$ of HKUST-1 were $2.1\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ and $1.5\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ for embryos and adults of zebrafish, respectively.[61] Therefore, the toxicity profiles of MOFs for gene delivery requires a more comprehensive evaluation.
Formulation of a MOF for therapeutic or diagnostic use must also address its physicochemical and colloidal stability as a delivery system. Some MOFs are unstable in physiological conditions, for example, cyclodextrin metal-organic frameworks (CD-MOFs) rapidly disintegrate when exposed to humid conditions.[62] The poor stability may cause decomposition of MOFs, premature drug release, and short circulation time, which greatly limits their biomedical application.[18] Furthermore, improving colloidal stability to prevent aggregation of MOFs during their systemic circulation is another issue that needs to be solved, because aggregation of NPs can be toxic per se.[18] It is worth mentioning that the stability of MOFs could be improved by surface modification with functional molecules.[17d,56b,63] Surface functionalization with nucleic acid, PEG, peptides, selected chemical compounds, polymers, and silica can increase the stability of MOFs and help achieve a targeted delivery.[23,64] In this regard, the effect of MOF surface modification on loading efficiency of nucleic acids needs addressing.
Demonstrating the in vivo fate of MOF-delivered gene therapy in animal models is an essential prerequisite to further justification for clinical trials. However, although some reports have studied the biodistribution of MOFs in major organs by imaging methods, the fate of oligonucleotide-MOF composition in vivo requires more detailed analysis. For example, the pharmacokinetic and pharmacodynamic processes determine the accumulation of oligonucleotides/MOFs in various tissues after administration.[65] Similarly, premature clearance of MOFbased NPs will adversely affect optimal payload delivery.[64a] Surface functionalization and size control are effective methods to improve the circulation time of MOFs.[64a,c,66] Biodegradability of the MOFs once the payload is delivered is also an important design feature to avoid MOF cumulative toxicity. Multimodal techniques have been utilised to monitor the degradation process of MOFs, including UV–vis,[67] nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy (NMR),[67] optical microscope,[68] laser scanning confocal microscope,[68] and atomic force microscopy.[69] The degradation pathways of a manganese-based MOF have been modelled using computational calculations.[70] However, further efforts are necessary to fully understand the real-world biological processes involved.
In summary, the numerous studies cited in this review support the use of MOFs as potential nonviral vectors for gene therapy and detection. Nucleic acid loaded MOFs effectively deliver the genetic materials with beneficial activity in vitro and in vivo. The MOF advantages of high payload capacity, biocompatibility and facile functionalization support the justification for continued efforts to develop MOF-nucleic acid nanoparticles with none of the challenges observed using viral vectors. The discovery and use of MOFs based on organic materials (e.g., amino acids,[57c,71] peptides,[58] nucleic acids,[56] fatty acids) to produce physiologically compatible frameworks will assist preclinical development and clinical use of these remarkable materials for gene as well as drug delivery.
